Alembic migrations for this project's two tables (users, loads).

How migrations get applied — never by the app itself, always by whatever
starts it:
  - Docker: backend/entrypoint.sh runs `alembic upgrade head` (with retry)
    before starting uvicorn.
  - CI: .github/workflows/ci.yml runs it as its own step before pytest.
  - pytest: a session-scoped autouse fixture in tests/conftest.py runs it
    once before any test — so `docker compose up -d db && pytest` just
    works, no separate manual step.
  - Bare local dev: run `alembic upgrade head` yourself once after
    `docker compose up -d db`, before `uvicorn app.main:app --reload`.

Writing a new migration, after changing app/models.py:
  cd backend
  alembic revision --autogenerate -m "add whatever column"

Then read the generated file before committing it — autogenerate gets the
table/column DDL right but not everything. In particular: dropping a table
does not drop the Postgres ENUM types its columns used (role, status are
both sa.Enum) — downgrade() needs an explicit
sa.Enum(name="...").drop(op.get_bind(), checkfirst=True)
for each, or the next upgrade() fails with "type already exists". See the
initial migration (versions/*_initial_schema.py) for the pattern — found
by actually running upgrade -> downgrade -> upgrade in a round trip while
building it, not by reading the autogenerated diff.

env.py builds its engine from app.core.database.engine directly (the same
DATABASE_URL resolution the app itself uses) rather than duplicating the
URL logic — alembic.ini deliberately has no sqlalchemy.url of its own.
